From: rgslide-linux <noreply@rgslide>
Subject: [PATCH] sipc/smem: use non-cached WC mapping for SRAM regions

In Linux 6.18, memremap() checks MEMREMAP_WB before MEMREMAP_WC. Passing
MEMREMAP_WC | MEMREMAP_WB for non-RAM shared regions (such as AON IRAM at
0x6500d000 used by the CM4 sensorhub SIPC sbuf rings) silently falls back
to write-back cached mappings, leaving AP ring-header writes stuck in the
CPU cache and invisible to the Cortex-M4.

Drop MEMREMAP_WB when mtype != MMAP_CACHE so SRAM regions are mapped with
MEMREMAP_WC (ioremap_wc).
---
 sipc/smem.c | 4 ++--
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/sipc/smem.c b/sipc/smem.c
index 1111111..2222222 100644
--- a/sipc/smem.c
+++ b/sipc/smem.c
@@ -181,9 +181,9 @@ static void *soc_modem_ram_vmap(phys_addr_t start, size_t size, E_MMAP_TYPE mtyp
 		}
 	}
 	map->vmapped = vaddr != NULL;
-	if (!vaddr)	/* no struct pages (a real no-map region): memremap can still map it */
+	if (!vaddr)	/* no struct pages (a real no-map / SRAM region): do not fall back to cached WB */
 		vaddr = memremap(page_start, page_count * PAGE_SIZE,
-				 mtype == MMAP_CACHE ? MEMREMAP_WB : MEMREMAP_WC | MEMREMAP_WB);
+				 mtype == MMAP_CACHE ? MEMREMAP_WB : MEMREMAP_WC);
 	if (!vaddr) {
 		pr_err("smem: cannot map %pa (%u pages)\n", &page_start, page_count);
 		kfree(map);
-- 
2.43.0
